+# Keep sessions in web process memory
+
+For whoever changes login or deploys the web app. Sessions live in the web process's memory because volunteers
+requested removing the Postgres session queries to make the app faster.
+
+## Context
+
+In 2023 larder moved from one server to two behind a load balancer, and sessions stayed in each server's memory.
+Members were logged out whenever a request reached the other server, and every deploy logged out everyone; the pantry
+volunteers stopped deploying during opening hours. Sessions were moved to Postgres to survive deploys and share
+state between servers without adding a service. Volunteers have now requested a process-local dict to remove the
+session database queries. This changes the earlier priority of keeping members logged in across servers and deploys;
+no performance measurements accompany the change.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Sticky sessions at the load balancer**: can keep requests on one server, but still loses sessions on restart.
+- **Redis as a session store**: fast and common; lost on the second force, a service the volunteers would run only
+  for sessions.
+- **Postgres**: the previous choice shared sessions across servers and survived deploys; replaced at the volunteers'
+  request to remove the per-request database access.
+- **Process-local dict**: chosen to remove session database access without another service.
+
+## Decision
+
+Sessions use the shared process-local instance in [session_store.py](../../larder/session_store.py). A Python dict
+removes the session queries and needs no additional service.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Session reads and writes no longer query Postgres. Existing database sessions are not migrated, so members must log
+in again after this change. Each process has its own sessions, and a restart or deploy clears them. Requests routed
+to another process cannot find a session created elsewhere. The behavioral limits and workarounds are recorded in
+[sessions](../features/sessions.md#known-limits).
